      "raw_payload": {
        "id": "R1",
        "kind": "hypothesis",
        "claim": "Separate seating from restraint: plywood-derived corner datums and an axial stop locate each rod; hand-operated M5 pressure members then press it into those datums. Tether movable members and nuts with cord, using loops for retention. This is a construction candidate, not demonstrated performance.",
        "delta": "Change the dependency from tight enclosing clearance to deliberate datum seating before clamping. The nearest rival is a tighter enclosing fit that restrains movement but may trap the rod before it reaches its stop.",
        "prediction": "If incomplete seating explains the supplied reversal, datum-first seating should reduce unloaded return error versus enclosing contact; tightening may improve loaded stability without improving return error. If both configurations seat equally yet return similarly, that explanation loses support. The 18 mm substitution may expose centerline or pointer-offset dependence despite repeatable face seating.",
        "probe": "Within the six plywood pieces, fabricate one reversible fixture with corner/stop contact and optional enclosing contact; use only supplied fasteners and tools. First verify retained components, hand access, 90 mm envelope and achievable fastening geometry. Compare two configurations with five exchanges of each original rod followed by two 18 mm substitutions each. Record exchange duration and unloaded pointer error. Use four ten-second 10 N trials per configuration, covering initial assembly, fifth exchange of each original rod and final substitution. Record target escape during loading. At forty seconds per exchange, only 160 seconds remain for switching, load setup and logging within twenty minutes; infeasible timing leaves the comparison unresolved.",
        "fit": "uncertain",
        "assumptions": [
          "Required rod orientation and pointer attachment permit fixed face datums; neither is supplied.",
          "Plywood subdivisions, eight bolts, four washers, loops and cord can realize adequately stiff, captive pressure members inside the envelope within 100 minutes; this requires verification.",
          "Defeat conditions include inconsistent stop contact, contact crushing, clamp slip, angular amplification beyond 2 mm, substitution-dependent offset, loose components, or exchanges exceeding forty seconds.",
          "Two adverse trials motivate a seating hypothesis but do not establish its cause."
        ],
        "source_refs": [
